A string is immutable, index-based and iterable

str = "Hello"
str[0] - means H
str[0] = "W" - cannot do that

my_dict = {}
other_dict = dict()
print(my_dict == {})        # Output: True
print(other_dict == {})    # Output: True

thisdict = {
  "brand": "Ford",
  "model": "Mustang",
  "year": 1964
}
x = thisdict["model"]
x = thisdict.get("model")
thisdict.keys()
thisdict.update({"year": 2020})
thisdict.pop("model")


-- loop through dictionary
for x in thisdict.keys():
    print(x)

for x in thisdict.values():
    print(x)

for x in thisdict.items():
    print(x)

## You cannot copy a dictionary simply by typing dict2 = dict1, because: dict2 will only be a reference to dict1, and changes made in dict1 will automatically also be made in dict2.

mydict = thisdict.copy()
mydict = dict(thisdict)

## Nested dictionary
child1 = {
  "name" : "Emil",
  "year" : 2004
}
child2 = {
  "name" : "Tobias",
  "year" : 2007
}
child3 = {
  "name" : "Linus",
  "year" : 2011
}

myfamily = {
  "child1" : child1,
  "child2" : child2,
  "child3" : child3
}
print(myfamily["child2"]["name"])

Loop through nested dictionary
for x, obj in myfamily.items():
  print(x)

  for y in obj:
    print(y + ':', obj[y])

-------------------------
Why is dictionary lookup O(1) on average?

A dictionary uses a hash table.
Each key is passed through a hash function which maps it to an index in memory.
Because this index is computed directly, lookup does not require scanning elements, making it O(1) on average.

Collisions can happen

Python resolves collisions using open addressing

Worst case can be O(n), but with a good hash function and resizing, it’s rare

📌 Final one-liner:

“Dictionary lookup is O(1) on average due to direct index access via hashing.”